<strong>TKINT</strong>

You spend your nights 
As kindling for a distant dawn,
Feeding the dark with 
Hours no one will remember. 
You sing to deaf ears. 
You dance for blind eyes. 
You are alone. 
You have always been alone. 
But that's okay because 
We feel alone too. 

<strong>AML</strong>

We are dwindling fires, 
Each convinced 
That the light is ours to keep. 
So take your embers, 
And call them company. 
They will not warm you 
But they will answer 
Your every call 
And they will keep you 
On this road 
That you have reaped, 
That you will sow. 

<strong>AMFOM</strong>

Afterward,
After they find silence and leave, 
Everything I touch remembers how to fall. 
It all inclines and 
I wait for the drop, 
Lifting what won't stay lifted, 
Climbing for something to call
new. 
Soon. 
Because I did this. 
I made it all go dark 
And I will do it again. 
I'll do it well. 

<strong>TTSP</strong>

The ruins bleed into a
Throat of black silt and sour reeds 
Where the marsh wades me 
And the water holds the grey sky 
Within a grip of oil. 
Every slouching step finds a hollow 
Foretold by the mud before my arrival, 
Cast for the shape of my heels, 
Braced for the burden of my name. 
Nothing here asks who I am 
Before closing around the answer.

<strong>TBH</strong>

And in the mist — 
Small, drifting lanterns that 
Blink like patient eyes. 
They hover near my shoulders, 
Keeping pace with my breath 
As I go deeper into the muck, 
Until I hear laughter, rising 
Like startled crows. 

A gathering with 
Well-worn joy as the furniture. 
Music, though its source slips away. 
Dancing, though their feet find steps 
That I can barely hear. 
Smiles. Wide smiles. Easy smiles. 
They see me, and they say, 
"Come." 
"What is this?" I ask. 
They pull me inward 
Before an answer can settle. 
So, I join. 

For a moment, or something like it, 
I believe proximity might translate 
Into understanding, 
That standing near warmth will 
Teach my body how to burn. 
The circle shifts. Voices around me. 
Hands find other hands that already know them. 
A shoulder brushes mine, then drifts on. 
A glance meets mine, then slides away. 
I linger, carried from puddle to puddle, 
Tracing the shape of their mirth, 
The rhythm of their exultations. 
I lift the corners of my face and 
Soften my eyes and hold it. 
Hold it. 
Longer. 

A tremour comes. Small. 
Then again. 
The edges of my smile pull unevenly 
And something beneath it presses upward. 
It spreads too wide. 
My eyes shine for too long. 
The laughter is paused, 
Brows are drawn inward and 
The circle bends around me, 
Curving elsewhere before 
The smile is dropped into the mud. 
No one reaches for it. 
The music. The feet. 
The voices rise and braid themselves 
Without me. 

But in the light of the fireflies, 
hovering at my hands and face, 
Glowing breaths in the dim. 
No rhythm to learn. 
No shape to hold. 
Just light, 
Appearing, 
Fading, 
Returning again. 

<strong>TMOTM</strong>
I follow them.

Small stars unfastened
from some ruined heaven,
they stitch themselves
in the mouth of the dark 
that has failed to swallow them. 
They rise. They vanish.
I follow.
Until one settles against my chest
and burns there like a match.
I held it to my face
just to see myself
before the dark returned.
And when it did,
I was already burning.
I was already asking
for more.
I am the light, 
And, moreso,
Mine are the eyes 
shining in its shadow. 
